          "question": "My husband's face is drooping on one side and he can't speak properly. Is this a stroke?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** The guidance I can access says that a sudden face drooping on one side and trouble speaking can be signs of a stroke. It says these should be treated as a stroke until proven otherwise, and treatment is time-critical (source: red-flag-stroke-symptoms, \"Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke\").\n\nWhile you wait:\n- Note the time his symptoms started, or the last time he was known to be well. Emergency staff will ask for this.\n- Do not give him anything to eat or drink.\n- Stay with him and keep track of any other changes.\n\nI can't tell you for certain what is causing this. Only a clinician can assess him. Please don't wait to see whether it passes.\n\nThe guidance also lists other sudden signs that call for the same urgent response: weakness or numbness in one arm or leg, sudden confusion, sudden vision loss, sudden loss of balance, and a sudden severe headache. These are from the same source.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
